/* WIDGET STAT BLOCKS — wrap at two per row instead of overflowing the card.
 *
 * homepage lays a widget's stat blocks out as a non-wrapping `flex-row` inside a card that
 * carries Tailwind's `overflow-clip`, so a row wider than the card is SILENTLY CUT OFF on the
 * right rather than wrapped or scrolled. Karakeep's four stats needed 335px in a 272px card:
 * 63px of "HIGHLIGHTS" simply vanished. Nothing reports this — no console error, no pod log,
 * and the tile keeps rendering.
 *
 * Two stats per row rather than three or a content-based fit, because it also EQUALISES TILE
 * HEIGHT, which three does not. Every widget here carries two, three or four stats, so at two
 * per row they all occupy exactly two stat rows and every widgeted tile lands on the same
 * height. Measured 2026-09-09 at 1920px: 13 widgeted tiles, all 172px, against 120px/136px
 * before. `.services-list` is a CSS grid, so tiles already stretch to their row's tallest
 * member — making the content uniform is what makes the stretch invisible.
 *
 * `overflow-wrap: anywhere` is the backstop for a narrow viewport. At 1280px a single long
 * label ("BOOKMARKS") still overran its half-width block by 7px, and a one-word label has no
 * break opportunity of its own. It engages only when a label genuinely does not fit, so it
 * changes nothing at 1920px.
 *
 * The `w-10` rule is the coinmarketcap widget's percent-change column: a fixed 2.5rem against
 * "-0.46%", which needs 43px. Negative values with two decimals clipped by 3px.
 *
 * #my-calendar is excluded throughout — it is a single full-width child laid out as a column
 * by the rules below, and a 50% flex-basis has no business there.
 *
 * VERIFY BY MEASURING, not by looking: `scrollWidth > clientWidth` on every descendant of
 * `li.service` finds a clipped block; the tile's own `<li>` does NOT show it, because the
 * clipping happens on the `div.service-card` inside it. Checking the `<li>` is what let this
 * ship in the first place.
 */
/* THE PERSONAL LINKS RENDER AS ICON ONLY.
 *
 * `#my-calendar ~ li.service` is every tile AFTER the calendar in its own group — the five
 * personal links, and nothing else on the page. A general-sibling selector is what scopes this,
 * so a tile added to any other group is unaffected.
 *
 * `a:has(> .service-name)` rather than `a:last-child`: `.service-title` holds THREE children —
 * the icon anchor, the name anchor, and a status-dot div that is last. Targeting `:last-child`
 * hides the dot container and leaves the name, which is what the first attempt did.
 *
 * The name and description stay in services.yaml.j2 rather than being deleted. Both feed
 * quicklaunch, which searches descriptions (`searchDescriptions: true` in settings.yaml.j2), so
 * the links stay keyboard-reachable by name and by domain while showing neither.
 *
 * THE NAME ANCHOR IS STRETCHED OVER THE TILE, NOT HIDDEN — it is what makes the whole tile
 * clickable. `display: none` on it (how this shipped first) leaves only the 48x32 icon anchor
 * as a hit target inside a 165px tile, so most of the tile looks like a button and does
 * nothing. Homepage renders the name inside its own `<a>` carrying the same href as the icon,
 * so covering the card with that anchor and hiding only the TEXT gives a tile that is entirely
 * a link. The card is already `position: relative`, so `inset: 0` resolves to it.
 *
 * Measured 2026-09-09 at 1920px: five 48px tiles on one row, icons centred, no clipping — and
 * again after this change, with `document.elementFromPoint` at each tile's centre and its four
 * edges returning the right href at every point on all ten tiles.
 */
#my-calendar ~ li.service .service-title > a:has(> .service-name) {
  display: flex;
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
}

#my-calendar ~ li.service .service-name {
  display: none;
}

#my-calendar ~ li.service .service-title {
  justify-content: center;
}

/* A TILE'S NAME AND DESCRIPTION WRAP RATHER THAN CLIP.
 *
 * Same trap as the stat blocks below: the card carries Tailwind's `overflow-clip`, so a title
 * row wider than the tile is cut off on the right with nothing logged. It bit the Monarch link
 * at five columns — `app.monarch.com` wanted 170px in a 165px tile and lost 5px.
 *
 * This is a guard, not the fix for that one tile: the description there was shortened instead,
 * because wrapping costs the whole grid row 16px of height. The rule only engages when a name
 * or description genuinely does not fit, which is what makes it safe to leave in place for the
 * next tile someone adds.
 */
li.service .service-title .service-name {
  min-width: 0;
  overflow-wrap: anywhere;
}

/* THE CARD MUST FILL THE TILE, or one sparse widget still looks different.
 *
 * `.services-list` is a CSS grid, so every `<li>` in a row is stretched to the row's tallest
 * member — but the visible `div.service-card` INSIDE it is not, and that card is what a reader
 * sees. FreshRSS carries two stats where every other widget carries three or four, so it
 * occupied one stat row instead of two: its `<li>` measured 172px like the rest while its card
 * measured 112px against their 164px. Measuring the `<li>` says the tiles match; measuring the
 * card says one of them is two thirds the height of its neighbours.
 *
 * `height: 100%` makes the card take the height the grid already gave the `<li>`, and the
 * column flex lets the stat container absorb the difference. `align-content: flex-start` keeps
 * the stat ROWS at their natural 44px — without it a single-row widget stretches that row to
 * fill the card and FreshRSS's blocks come out 104px against everyone else's 44px, which trades
 * one inconsistency for another. Measured 2026-09-09 at 1920px: all nine cards 172px, every
 * block 44x128.
 */
li.service:not(#my-calendar) > div.service-card {
  height: 100%;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
}

li.service:not(#my-calendar) .service-container {
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  align-content: flex-start;
}

li.service:not(#my-calendar) .service-container > div {
  /* NO GROW — the `0` in the first position is the whole point. With `flex-grow: 1` an odd
   * stat count left the last block stretched across the full row: the five three-stat widgets
   * rendered 128px, 128px, then a 264px block, against a uniform 128px on the four-stat ones.
   * Same tile height, visibly different blocks. Growth disabled, every block is 128px and an
   * odd count simply leaves the last half-row empty, which reads as a grid rather than as a
   * mistake. Measured 2026-09-09 at 1920px across all nine Services tiles. */
  flex: 0 0 calc(50% - 0.5rem);
  min-width: 0;
}

/* A THREE-STAT WIDGET SPANS ITS THREE BOXES ACROSS THE FULL WIDTH.
 *
 * At two per row an odd count leaves the last box alone on a half-empty row. Two and four
 * divide evenly; three does not. No widget carries three stats since the 2026-09-10 field
 * pruning, so this rule matches nothing today — it stays because it costs nothing and applies
 * itself the moment a widget's stat count lands on three again.
 *
 * `:has(> :nth-child(3):last-child)` is "exactly three children": a third child that is also
 * the last. It matches nothing on a two- or four-stat widget, so those keep the 50% basis
 * above.
 *
 * Measured 2026-09-09 at 1920px: three boxes of 83px on one row, against 128px for the 2- and
 * 4-stat widgets. At 1280px they are 54px and the labels wrap rather than clip, which is what
 * the `overflow-wrap` rule below is for.
 */
li.service:not(#my-calendar) .service-container:has(> :nth-child(3):last-child) > div {
  flex-basis: calc(33.333% - 0.5rem);
}

/* A ONE-STAT WIDGET SPANS ITS SINGLE BOX ACROSS THE FULL WIDTH.
 *
 * Same reasoning as the three-stat rule above, at the other end: at a 50% basis a lone box sits
 * in the left half of the card with dead space beside it, which reads as a stat that failed to
 * load rather than as a tile with one number. FreshRSS is the only one — `fields: [unread]`.
 *
 * `:has(> :first-child:last-child)` is "exactly one child".
 *
 * `calc(100% - 0.5rem)`, NOT `100%`, and the subtraction is the same one the 50% and 33.3% bases
 * make: a block carries `m-1`, so 8px of horizontal margin sits outside the basis. At a flat
 * `100%` the block came to 280px inside a 272px container and the card's `overflow-clip` cut the
 * last 4px off — measured on the deployed page 2026-09-10, the only clipped element on the page.
 * A basis that ignores the margin overflows by exactly that margin, which is small enough to
 * read as a rendering quirk rather than as a bug.
 */
li.service:not(#my-calendar) .service-container:has(> :first-child:last-child) > div {
  flex-basis: calc(100% - 0.5rem);
}

/* RESERVE ONE STAT ROW IN EVERY WIDGET, so a sparser widget does not shrink its whole grid row.
 *
 * `.services-list` sizes each grid row to its tallest member, and `height: 100%` above makes
 * every card fill that. Both are per-ROW, so a row whose tiles all hold fewer stat rows than
 * the rows below it shrinks on its own and the column reads uneven down its length.
 *
 * ONE row, not two, since the 2026-09-10 field pruning: no widget on this dashboard carries
 * more than two stats now, and two fit one row at the 50% basis above. Reserving two left an
 * empty 44px band in every card. 3.25rem is one stat row — a block is 44px, plus its margins.
 *
 * A widget that later carries three or four stats wraps to a second row and takes its grid row
 * with it, which is correct: the reservation is a floor, not a cap. It also takes the calendar
 * beside it out of alignment, because that height is derived from this row height.
 */
li.service:not(#my-calendar) .service-container {
  min-height: 3.25rem;
}

li.service:not(#my-calendar) .service-container > div > div {
  overflow-wrap: anywhere;
}

li.service:not(#my-calendar) .service-container .font-bold.w-10 {
  width: auto;
  min-width: 3.25rem;
}

/* BLOCK LABELS ARE RENAMED IN CSS, BECAUSE HOMEPAGE OFFERS NO OTHER WAY.
 *
 * A block's heading comes from `t(label)` in src/components/services/widget/block.jsx — an i18n
 * lookup against public/locales/<lang>/common.json, which is baked into the image. No config key
 * overrides it: `fields:` selects WHICH blocks render, never what they are called. The two
 * alternatives were both worse. Mounting a replacement common.json means shipping the whole
 * upstream file and re-reconciling it on every image bump, for four words. Rewriting these tiles
 * as `customapi` widgets, which do take a `label:`, means re-deriving each service's numbers from
 * its raw API — and for uptimekuma that is a count the widget computes and the API does not
 * expose.
 *
 * `font-size: 0` rather than `display: none` or `visibility: hidden`: the box must keep its
 * place in the flex column, and it must be sized by the REPLACEMENT text. "Battery Charge" wraps
 * to two lines in an 85px block at 1280px, which pushed its whole grid row to 136px against the
 * 120px of every other row — measured on the deployed page 2026-09-10. `visibility: hidden`
 * would have kept that wrap and the extra 16px with it.
 *
 * TILES ARE MATCHED BY HREF, NOT BY POSITION. A `:nth-child` on the list would silently mislabel
 * a tile the next reorder — and the links above were reordered the same day. The block index
 * within a tile is still positional, because homepage renders a widget's blocks in the order its
 * component declares them; that order is the `fields:` list in services.yaml.j2, and the two are
 * ENFORCED to agree by ansible/tests/services/test_homepage_block_label_overrides.py.
 */
li.service:has(a[href*="uptime-kuma."]) .service-block:nth-child(1) > .font-bold,
li.service:has(a[href*="uptime-kuma."]) .service-block:nth-child(2) > .font-bold,
li.service:has(a[href*="speedtest."]) .service-block:nth-child(1) > .font-bold,
li.service:has(a[href*="karakeep."]) .service-block:nth-child(1) > .font-bold,
li.service:has(a[href*="peanut."]) .service-block:nth-child(1) > .font-bold,
li.service:has(a[href*="peanut."]) .service-block:nth-child(2) > .font-bold,
li.service:has(a[href*="qBittorrent."]) .service-block:nth-child(2) > .font-bold {
  font-size: 0;
}

li.service:has(a[href*="uptime-kuma."]) .service-block:nth-child(1) > .font-bold::after,
li.service:has(a[href*="uptime-kuma."]) .service-block:nth-child(2) > .font-bold::after,
li.service:has(a[href*="speedtest."]) .service-block:nth-child(1) > .font-bold::after,
li.service:has(a[href*="karakeep."]) .service-block:nth-child(1) > .font-bold::after,
li.service:has(a[href*="peanut."]) .service-block:nth-child(1) > .font-bold::after,
li.service:has(a[href*="peanut."]) .service-block:nth-child(2) > .font-bold::after,
li.service:has(a[href*="qBittorrent."]) .service-block:nth-child(2) > .font-bold::after {
  /* text-xs, the class the label carries — the parent's 0 would otherwise be inherited. */
  font-size: 0.75rem;
  line-height: 1rem;
}

/* Sites Up -> Up, Sites Down -> Down */
li.service:has(a[href*="uptime-kuma."]) .service-block:nth-child(1) > .font-bold::after {
  content: "Up";
}
li.service:has(a[href*="uptime-kuma."]) .service-block:nth-child(2) > .font-bold::after {
  content: "Down";
}

/* Download -> Down. Ping keeps its own label. */
li.service:has(a[href*="speedtest."]) .service-block:nth-child(1) > .font-bold::after {
  content: "Down";
}

/* Bookmarks -> Saved. Archived keeps its own label. */
li.service:has(a[href*="karakeep."]) .service-block:nth-child(1) > .font-bold::after {
  content: "Saved";
}

/* Battery Charge -> Charge, UPS Status -> Status */
li.service:has(a[href*="peanut."]) .service-block:nth-child(1) > .font-bold::after {
  content: "Charge";
}
li.service:has(a[href*="peanut."]) .service-block:nth-child(2) > .font-bold::after {
  content: "Status";
}

/* Download -> Down, matching the Speedtest tile. The href here is `qBittorrent.<domain>` with
 * that capital B, and an attribute substring match is case-sensitive. */
li.service:has(a[href*="qBittorrent."]) .service-block:nth-child(2) > .font-bold::after {
  content: "Down";
}

/* THE CALENDAR SPANS ITS WHOLE GROUP.
 *
 * The `Calendar` group is five columns wide (settings.yaml.j2) so the five personal links fill
 * one row beneath the calendar. `columns` is uniform across a group, so without this the
 * calendar itself would render a fifth of the width. Measured 2026-09-09 at 1920px: 856px wide
 * with this rule, five links at 171px each on the row below, column total 456px.
 */
#my-calendar {
  grid-column: 1 / -1;
}

/* EVERY GROUP'S ROWS GET VERTICAL BREATHING ROOM, AND THE CARD STOPS PAYING FOR IT.
 *
 * homepage's grid sets no row gap and separates rows with an 8px BOTTOM MARGIN on each card
 * instead. That margin is outside the card but inside the grid item, so the `<li>` measures 8px
 * taller than its card — and `height: 100%` above then stretches the card back over that 8px,
 * which the `flex: 1 1 auto` stat container absorbs. Measured on the deployed page 2026-09-10 at
 * 1920px: a 120px card holding 8px of padding, a 52px title and a 52px stat row in a 60px
 * container, with three grid rows at y=168, 288 and 408 — exactly 120px apart, so the rows
 * touched.
 *
 * Moving the separation to the grid's own `row-gap` fixes both at once. The card loses its
 * margin and shrinks to the 112px it actually needs, the gap becomes a real 12px, and the two
 * are no longer the same 8px doing both jobs badly.
 *
 * This applies to every group, where the previous version scoped it to the calendar's. A group
 * that renders one row is unaffected at 1920px and separates properly when a narrow viewport
 * wraps it.
 */
ul.services-list {
  row-gap: 0.75rem;
}

li.service > div.service-card {
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

/* THE CALENDAR'S HEIGHT IS DERIVED FROM THE SERVICES COLUMN BESIDE IT.
 *
 * The two Top Row columns are separate grids, so nothing makes them end level on its own — the
 * personal links hung 108px below the Services block. They are also not siblings in one grid
 * row, so `height: 100%` on the calendar's list does not match it to the Services list: tried on
 * the deployed page 2026-09-10, the calendar column GREW to 676px against Services' 360px,
 * because the height it resolved against was its own.
 *
 * So the calendar is pinned, and the pin is computed rather than typed. The Calendar column is
 * the calendar plus two rows of links; the Services column is four rows of widget cards. Setting
 * them equal and solving for the calendar leaves the `calc()` below, which reads as the same
 * sentence: four widget rows, minus the two link rows, minus one row gap of difference.
 *
 * At 1920px that is 4x112 + 3x12 - (2x40 + 2x12) = 380px, and both columns end at y=652.
 *
 * THE THREE MEASUREMENTS ARE NOT ARBITRARY, but neither are they enforced — they are what the
 * cards come out at, so a change to the tile content can move them. --widget-row is a card's
 * height (4px padding twice, a 52px title row, a 52px stat row); --link-row is an icon-only
 * card. Re-measure both if a tile grows, and re-derive if the Services tile count leaves four
 * rows: `getBoundingClientRect().bottom` on the last tile of each column is the check.
 */
:root {
  --widget-row: 7rem; /* 112px */
  --link-row: 2.5rem; /* 40px */
  --services-rows: 4;
}

#my-calendar {
  height: calc(
    var(--services-rows) * var(--widget-row) + 0.75rem - 2 * var(--link-row)
  );
}

#my-calendar > div.service-card {
  height: 100%;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
}

#my-calendar .service-container {
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  min-height: 0;
}

#my-calendar .service-container > div {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  height: 100%;
  min-height: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
}

/* Target the events list directly - it's the last div inside the calendar content */
#my-calendar .service-container > div > div:last-child {
  overflow-y: auto;
  min-height: 0;
}

#my-calendar .service-container > div > div:last-child::-webkit-scrollbar {
  width: 4px;
}
#my-calendar .service-container > div > div:last-child::-webkit-scrollbar-track {
  background: transparent;
}
#my-calendar .service-container > div > div:last-child::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb {
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.15);
  border-radius: 2px;
}
